}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535531 true] [17592186353893 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353894 13194139535531 true] [17592186353893 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535531 true] [17592186353893 1041 "field-1" 13194139535531 true] [17592186353893 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353894 13194139535531 true] [17592186353893 1110 #uuid "46fb9669-d1f3-5edd-87dd-8618c92c7f74" 13194139535531 true] [17592186353894 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}\\sec(2\\theta)^3" 13194139535531 true] [17592186353894 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535531 true] [17592186353894 1111 #uuid "00ce826b-1a03-5e05-a993-0e8e5e8fec3d" 13194139535531 true] [17592186353895 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353896 13194139535531 true] [17592186353895 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353898 13194139535531 true] [17592186353895 1089 "q-352489" 13194139535531 true] [17592186353895 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535531 true] [17592186353895 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/50ed8b3093adf431f41e6332fa43ac122df79c98d487961037443c77b4290fb7.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above, calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$ Fill in the missing parts in the result below.\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" 13194139535531 true] [17592186353895 1113 #uuid "b21b8bfe-2104-5b14-b103-89256b6fdf9c" 13194139535531 true] [17592186353895 1145 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, 